{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"diet-capsizable-sparrow","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\n\n`infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/handler.py` converts CloudWatch token counts\ninto dollars through a rate table derived daily from Cost Explorer and matched to\nmodels by string heuristics. That layer has produced four distinct defects in the\ntwelve days since the cap was deployed (2026-09-12), two of which reached\nproduction and neither of which raised an alarm:\n\n1. `_rate_for`/`_match` direction-suffix collision. The candidate suffix\n   `input-token-count` also matches the rate key `...-cache-write-input-token-count`,\n   and the longest match wins, so a model's input rate resolves to its cache-write\n   rate. It fires if and only if the ModelId carries no version suffix, which is\n   live for `claude-sonnet-4-6`, `claude-opus-4-7`, `claude-opus-4-8` and\n   `claude-opus-5` — the majority of this account's usage.\n2. `_normalise` mismatch. Billing label `Nova2.0Lite` normalises to `nova20lite`\n   while model id `us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0` normalises to `nova2litev10`.\n   Neither contains the other, so an existing correct rate is not found and the\n   model falls through to `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`, which is set far above any real\n   rate.\n3. `BEDROCK_SERVICE_VALUES` is a hardcoded Cost Explorer service allowlist\n   (introduced by 3083-ada3-3a0f-4051). It omits `Claude Opus 4.8`, `Claude Opus 5`,\n   `Claude Opus 4.5`, `Cohere Rerank v3.5` and `Claude 3 Haiku`, so the\n   authoritative CE signal sees roughly a third of real Bedrock spend.\n4. `MARKETPLACE_TOKEN_SUFFIXES` handles only the CamelCase usage-type convention.\n   AWS bills newer marketplace models in snake_case (`USE1_input_tokens_standard-Units`)\n   and also publishes `_Global` variants; both are silently skipped by\n   `_refresh_rates`.\n\nProduction evidence: for seven consecutive days one model was priced at the\nfail-closed fallback rate, emitting 6,112 `fail-closed fallback` events into the\nLambda log group with no alert, while the cap's own logged estimate ran at roughly\ntwice settled billing. Separately, the marketplace-units defect fixed by Gerrit\n3032 had the cap reporting an estimate five orders of magnitude above reality.\nExact figures are deliberately omitted here — see \"Figures\" below.\n\nThese are not four bugs. They are one class: matching model identifiers against\nbilling labels with substring and suffix heuristics. The account has already\nproduced four different shapes of that mismatch, so the class will recur.\n\n## Approach\n\nDelete the pricing-derivation layer and price every token at a ceiling rate for\nits direction. The ceiling is set above the highest per-direction rate observed on\nthis account, so the estimate is an upper bound on true spend and the cap cannot\nfire late.\n\nEnforcement becomes, per UTC day:\n\n    spend = max( sum over regions and the four token metrics of\n                   undimensioned CloudWatch Sum * CEILING[metric],\n                 ce_spend )\n\nCost Explorer stays in the enforcement expression. It is the only signal covering\nBedrock spend that publishes no token metric at all (Cohere Rerank is a live\nexample; provisioned throughput, batch and model customization are structural\nones), and it is orthogonal to every defect above.\n\nCloudWatch reads move from per-ModelId enumeration to the four undimensioned\naggregates. Verified equivalent: over 2026-09-14 to 09-24 the undimensioned\naggregate and the sum of per-ModelId series agree to four decimal places, and the\naggregate is very slightly larger because `list_metrics` only returns series with\nrecent data.\n\n### Accepted limitation\n\nPricing every token at the ceiling over-estimates spend for models much cheaper\nthan the priciest one. For the cheapest models on this account the factor is two\norders of magnitude, and for two models that currently bill nothing for\ncache-write it is unbounded. A large embedding or small-model batch job can\ntherefore trip the cap at a small fraction of the intended spend. This is accepted\ndeliberately in favour of the simplest implementation, and must be stated in the\nREADME as a known limit. The remedy if it ever bites is an exact-match\n`{ModelId: rate}` override table defaulting to the ceiling — exact string equality\nonly, never substring or normalisation matching, so it cannot reopen this defect\nclass.\n\n## Figures\n\nMachine-local policy bars spend totals, unit costs, budgets, cap thresholds and\nbill figures from the tracker, because the tickets branch auto-pushes to the\npublic GitHub mirror. The ceiling constants, the threshold value, the observed\nrate table, the 50-day back-test distribution and the incident figures are\nrecorded in the operator's local-only design note at\n`~/Desktop/bedrock-cap-redesign-local-only/design.md`. Implement from that file.\nDo not copy any figure from it into this ticket, a comment, a session log, a\ncommit message or the README.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The enforcement expression contains no model-identifier matching and no\n   usage-type string parsing. Specifically, `_refresh_rates`, `_rate_for`,\n   `_seed_for`, `_normalise`, `_strip_billing_region`, `_marketplace_rate_key`\n   and `_rate_key_for_ce_group` are deleted from\n   `infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/handler.py`, along with the constants\n   `SEED_RATES`, `RATE_OVERRIDES`, `BEDROCK_SERVICE_VALUES`,\n   `MARKETPLACE_TOKEN_SUFFIXES`, `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`,\n   `MARKETPLACE_TOKENS_PER_RATE_UNIT` and the per-direction multipliers in\n   `TOKEN_METRICS`. A test asserts none of those names survives in the module.\n2. `_metered_spend` issues exactly four undimensioned `GetMetricData` queries per\n   configured region and performs no `list_metrics` call on the enforcement path.\n3. A `CEILING` mapping supplies one rate per token metric, taken from the\n   local-only design note. A test asserts each value is at least the highest rate\n   for that direction in a recorded Cost Explorer payload fixture.\n4. Enforcement still compares `max(ceiling_estimate, ce_spend)` against the\n   threshold. `_ce_spend_today` classifies Bedrock spend by predicate —\n   service equals `Amazon Bedrock` or ends with the Bedrock Edition suffix — not\n   by an enumerated allowlist. A test proves the predicate matches every Bedrock\n   service present in the recorded payload, including the five the old allowlist\n   omitted.\n5. The cap does not release on a tick where every CloudWatch query returned no\n   datapoints. Releasing requires a positive, non-empty reading below threshold.\n   A test covers the empty-read case and asserts the deny stays attached.\n6. The threshold and warning values in `infra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf` are\n   set to the values in the design note, chosen so the median effective cap lands\n   near the intended daily figure.\n7. `state[\"rates\"]`, `state[\"rates_day\"]` and `metered_estimated` are removed from\n   the state document and from the handler's return value, together with\n   `MeteredRate` and the `\" (estimated)\"` rendering. A test asserts the persisted\n   state document no longer carries a rate table.\n8. A daily guard compares the ceiling estimate for a settled day against Cost\n   Explorer for that same day and emits an SNS warning if the estimate is below\n   actual, i.e. the upper-bound invariant is violated. The guard cannot attach or\n   detach the deny policy.\n9. A daily `list_metrics` ModelId diff emits an SNS warning when a ModelId appears\n   that is not in a recorded known set, so a newly-invoked model is surfaced on\n   first use rather than after it is billed.\n10. On the warn and trip paths only, per-model token counts are logged for\n    attribution. No prices and no matching are involved.\n11. Tests are driven by recorded real Cost Explorer and `list_metrics` payloads\n    checked into the repository, not synthesized fixtures. The recorded CE payload\n    must contain CamelCase, snake_case and `_Global` usage types and a non-token\n    usage type in one file. A property test asserts every ModelId in the recorded\n    `list_metrics` payload is priced by the ceiling with no fallback path.\n12. The README documents the accepted over-estimate from criterion \"Accepted\n    limitation\", the resulting effective-cap band in qualitative terms, and the\n    fact that the estimate is an upper bound rather than an estimate of actual\n    spend.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nOptimistic concurrency on the SSM state write, and the `signal_unavailable`\nrelease deadlock. Fourteen days of logs show zero occurrences of either failure\nmode against roughly seven thousand pricing failures. Track separately if wanted.","id":"7775-46e5-3fd7-42d2","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["bedrock","cost","infra","simplification"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Replace the Bedrock spend cap's per-model pricing layer with a ceiling rate per token direction"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1790270993411121757,"uuid":"aa2f19f6-20f2-4d4e-ac65-49ffbd3bae64"}